// @run
// @log 1
// @log 4
// @hint redundant type annotation: `p` is inferred as `{a: i32, b: i32}[]`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `q` is inferred as `{a: i32, b: i32}[]`
// TWO INTERSECTION ALIASES OF ONE LAYOUT, BOTH AS REF LISTS (silent-class-inventory D441).
// Was check-clean INVALID WASM. The struct table minted one row per alias NAME — `AB` and
// `CD` got distinct WasmGC heap types for an identical layout — while the ref-list table
// dedups on LAYOUT and minted ONE array type, whose element is the FIRST row. So `q[0]`
// read a `(ref 0)` out of a `(array (mut (ref null 0)))` and `.b` applied `struct.get 1 1`
// to it. Two tables, two different keys, one program.
//
// The two rows failed to merge for the reason D402, D442 and D443 all share: `type X = A & B`
// canonicalizes to a ONE-VARIANT `UnionDecl`, so `internShapeAs`'s D0 recorder banked the
// ALIAS name's arena type — a union — `slotCanonId`'s `TyObj` gate declined it and returned
// the "never merged" -1, and `buildStructTwins` tests that key FIRST. Recording the SHAPE's
// arena type instead is the whole fix, and it is the same degenerate `sTyIxOfNameTy`'s
// generic-application arm was added for (`generics/genapp-instance-twins-declared.vl`).
//
// THE PLAIN-STRUCT TWIN of this program always ran (one heap type, one array type), which is
// what made the pair a defect rather than a limit: `int + plain`, `plain + int` and
// `plain + plain` all print 1 and 4.
type AB = {a: i32} & {b: i32}
type CD = {a: i32} & {b: i32}
const p: AB[] = [{ a: 1, b: 2 }]
const q: CD[] = [{ a: 3, b: 4 }]
print(p[0].a)
print(q[0].b)
